{"name":"Basil Icing","hex":"#e2e6db","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"basil","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant (Ocimum basilicum).","The leaves of this plant used as a herb."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The angle to which a joiner's tool is ground away."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/basil"},{"term":"icing","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sweet, often creamy and thick glaze made primarily of sugar, often enriched with ingredients like butter, egg whites, or flavorings, typically used for baked goods.","A minor violation of ice hockey rules, occurring when a player shoots the puck from his/her side of the red line so that it crosses the goal line on the opponent's side. A team playing short-handed is not penalized for this."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of ice"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/icing"}],"entries":[{"title":"Basil","blocks":[{"text":"Basil, also called great basil, is a culinary herb of the family Lamiaceae (mints). It is a tender plant and is used in cuisines worldwide. In Western cuisine, the generic term \"basil\" refers to the variety also known as Genovese basil or sweet basil. Basil is native to tropical and subtopical Asia and Australasia from India and Sri Lanka east to southeastern China, Taiwan, and south through Southeast Asia, the Philippines, New Guinea, to northern Australia. In temperate climates basil is treated as an annual plant, but it can be grown as a short-lived perennial or biennial in warmer horticultural zones with tropical or Mediterranean climates."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Ocimum_basilicum_8zz.jpg/330px-Ocimum_basilicum_8zz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basil","wikidata":"Q38859"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Ocimum_basi